# Sentinel frontend environment configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` (`.env` is gitignored) and adjust for your machine.
# The values are read ONLY in src/config.ts -- components never build URLs.
#
# REMEMBER TO RESTART VITE after editing this file: Vite only reads .env when
# the dev server boots, so a stale process keeps serving the old API URLs.
#
# WHICH HOST?
# -----------
# src/config.ts derives each API host from window.location.hostname when a
# VITE_*_API_URL is unset, so the browser always calls the same host it already
# reached the page on:
#
#     http://localhost:5173   ->  http://localhost:8081   (loopback workflow)
#     http://192.168.182.128:5173 -> http://192.168.182.128:8081  (LAN workflow)
#
# That is the robust default: no address is baked into the bundle, so a moved
# or re-addressed VM needs no rebuild.
#
# Pin the values below only when a specific address is wanted regardless of how
# the page was opened. An explicit value always wins over the derived one.
#
# Do NOT pin 127.0.0.1 in a LAN deployment: in a browser on another machine,
# 127.0.0.1 is that machine, not this VM, and every request fails with
# ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED.
#
# BIND ADDRESS (backend)
# ----------------------
# Deriving the URL is only half of it: the servers must listen on the LAN
# interface, not loopback. Start them with:
#
#   ANALYTICS_HOST=0.0.0.0 CONTROL_HOST=0.0.0.0 \
#     FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://192.168.182.128:5173 ./scripts/serve-backend.sh
#
# CORS
# ----
# The control plane (8000) allows any origin in development, so it works from a
# browser with no configuration. The analytics plane (8081) keeps a narrow
# loopback-only allow-list, so when this frontend is opened from anywhere other
# than localhost -- for example http://192.168.182.128:5173 -- the browser
# blocks every analytics response and the header shows Analytics as offline even
# though the API is healthy.
#
# Fix that on the backend when starting it, not here. This file cannot grant a
# browser permission; the server has to send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.
# FRONTEND_ORIGIN is appended to the derived allow-list by serve-backend.sh;
# no wildcard and no disabled CORS.
#
#   FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://192.168.182.128:5173 ./scripts/serve-backend.sh
#
# The analytics API answers the frontend in Vite dev mode only; there is no
# bundling of a production frontend in this repository.

# Server A -- read-only analytics plane (assessments, risk, findings, evidence, XAI, ML)
# Unset = derive from window.location.hostname. LAN deployment example:
# VITE_ANALYTICS_API_URL=http://192.168.182.128:8081

# Server B -- mutating control plane (experiment configurations, create/poll jobs)
# Unset = derive from window.location.hostname. LAN deployment example:
# VITE_CONTROL_API_URL=http://192.168.182.128:8000

# Server C -- read-only IPsec explanation plane ("Explain with AI" / "Ask AI")
#
# Optional. With nothing running here the UI shows a one-line "explanation
# service unavailable" and the assessment, findings and evidence stay fully
# usable; the AI panel is the only thing that is missing, and it never blocks
# the investigation.
#
#   python -m correlation.ai.service
#
# This plane explains and decides nothing. It is separate from Server A because
# the analytics plane is GET-only by contract.
# Unset = derive from window.location.hostname. LAN deployment example:
# VITE_AI_API_URL=http://192.168.182.128:8082

# Poll interval for a running experiment job, in milliseconds.
VITE_EXPERIMENT_POLL_MS=2000

# Request timeout for a single API call, in milliseconds.
VITE_API_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
